    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail for a simple reason: they are asked to do two jobs that pull in opposite directions. One job is to help a team decide what to build next. The other is to reassure people who are not on the team that the future is under control. The second job almost always wins, and once it does, the roadmap stops being a tool and becomes a performance.\n\nYou can see this in how roadmaps get made. Someone senior wants to know what is shipping in Q3. A product manager, wanting to look competent, fills in the boxes. Sales wants a date for the feature a big customer asked about, so a date appears. Engineering is asked to estimate work that has not been scoped, and they guess, knowing the guess will be treated as a commitment. By the time the document is finished, it is a list of promises made to people who each heard what they wanted to hear. Nobody lied, exactly. But nobody was really planning either.\n\nThe deeper problem is that a roadmap encodes a level of certainty that does not exist. Six months out, you do not know what customers will ask for, what a competitor will launch, which assumption in your current bet will turn out to be wrong, or which two engineers will leave. Everyone knows this. Yet the format of a roadmap, with its neat quarters and confident labels, quietly erases that knowledge. It looks like a schedule, so it gets treated like one. When reality diverges, which it always does, the roadmap is either abandoned or defended. Both outcomes are bad. Abandoned, it teaches the organization that planning is theater. Defended, it forces teams to ship things they already know are wrong because changing course would look like failure.\n\nRoadmaps also tend to list features instead of problems. A feature is easy to put on a timeline and easy to check off. A problem is messier, because you might discover halfway through that the feature you picked does not actually solve it. But a feature list gives a team no way to think. When the plan says \"build export to CSV\" and a user interview reveals that nobody actually wants CSV, they want to stop needing the export at all, the roadmap has no room for that finding. It was written to be executed, not to be learned from.\n\nAnd then there is capacity. Almost every roadmap I have seen assumes the team spends all of its time on roadmap items. It ignores bugs, support escalations, the migration that cannot wait, the thing the CEO saw at a conference. Unplanned work is not an exception; it is a fixed cost, usually a third of the team's time or more. A plan that pretends otherwise is late from the day it is published.\n\nNone of this means roadmaps are useless. A good one states what the team is trying to learn and what it currently believes, and it invites disagreement. It gets shorter and vaguer the further out it goes, because that is honest. The roadmaps that fail are the ones that were never allowed to be honest in the first place.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they are asked to do two incompatible jobs at once. They are supposed to be a strategic instrument, a way of saying what matters and what doesn't, and they are also supposed to be a promise, a schedule of things that will exist by certain dates. Strategy requires the freedom to change your mind when the world tells you something new. Promises require that you don't. Put both demands on a single document and it will quietly become a promise, because promises are what other people remember.\n\nThe first failure, then, is that roadmaps get treated as commitments by everyone except the people who wrote them. A sales leader sees a feature in the Q3 column and mentions it to a prospect. A support director tells an angry customer relief is coming in the spring. Marketing starts drafting the launch. By the time the product team learns something that should change the plan, the plan has been socialized into an obligation. Changing it now looks like failure rather than learning, so the team ships the thing anyway, late and diminished, and calls it a win.\n\nThe second failure is that roadmaps are usually lists of outputs rather than statements of intent. A row that says \"notifications revamp\" tells you nothing about what problem is being solved or how you would know if it worked. Teams building against output lists optimize for shipping, because shipping is the only thing being measured. You end up with a year of completed items and no discernible improvement in the business, which is how organizations arrive at the strange conclusion that they are executing well and losing anyway.\n\nThird, roadmaps are built on estimates that everyone knows are fiction and no one is allowed to say so. Effort guesses made before discovery are wrong by large multiples, and they are wrong in one direction. So the calendar is fantasy from the first day, and every subsequent week is spent negotiating which parts of the fantasy to abandon. This is exhausting and corrosive, and it teaches engineers that planning is theater.\n\nFourth, most roadmaps are aggregations rather than choices. Each stakeholder gets something, so nothing gets enough. The document becomes a peace treaty among departments, which is a perfectly reasonable political achievement and a terrible allocation of attention. Real roadmaps say no loudly, and saying no loudly is expensive for the person holding the pen, which is why it so rarely happens.\n\nWhat tends to work better is modesty about time and clarity about purpose. Be specific about what you are doing now, name the problem you expect to solve next and why it matters, and keep the far future deliberately vague, described in terms of the outcomes you are chasing rather than the features you imagine will produce them. Revisit it often enough that changing it feels normal. Tell the rest of the company how to read it, including which parts are firm and which are hypotheses.\n\nNone of this makes planning easy. It just stops the plan from lying.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
